//! check: fail(E0101)
//! phase: none
//! conforms: gram.lex.str.escape, gram.lex.str.multi
//!
//! Issue #215, the refusal half — le05's actual question, answered by a
//! file instead of by a contrast between prose bullets. `\q` is not an
//! escape wolf has; inside `"…"` that is **E0101**, and this pins that
//! it is E0101 inside `"""…"""` too, because `MULTI_PART` reaches the
//! same `STR_ESC` a plain string's `STR_PART` does. An
//! `invalid_escape` node therefore belongs inside a multiline: the
//! productions say so now, and this file measures it.
//!
//! Its running twin is `strings/multiline_escapes.lu`, which pins the
//! escapes that ARE derived. Same shape as #198's pair one literal
//! over (`grammar/str_uni_seven_digits.lu` and
//! `strings/str_uni_leading_zeros.lu`).
//!
//! It also RECORDS A DIVERGENCE, and #225 carries the numbers: lupin
//! 0.1.23 refuses this file under a DIFFERENT code — one the catalog
//! already spends on a multiline's opening line — and refuses a plain
//! `"\q"` under it too, so the divergence is about the escape, not the
//! multiline. E0101 is normative twice over: the catalog assigns it to
//! the invalid escape, and `[gram.lex.str.escape]` has said "any other
//! `\` is E0101 at the escape" since before this file. #198's two
//! witnesses agree because they pin the `\u{…}` DIGIT BOUND, which both
//! machines number the same; nothing had ever pinned an unknown escape,
//! which is how a code collision survived 484 differential entries.

fn main() -> !int {
    let s = """
        \q
        """
    if s == "" { 0 } else { 1 }
}
